      content: 'Key areas to assess in a child with myelomeningocele and new neurologic changes:\n- **Tethered cord symptoms**: New or worsening back pain (especially with flexion), progressive motor weakness, changes in gait pattern, new or worsening scoliosis, increasing foot deformity (cavovarus, equinus), deteriorating bladder or bowel function. Tethered cord occurs in 20-50% of children with repaired myelomeningocele.\n- **VP shunt function**: Headaches, nausea/vomiting, lethargy, vision changes, sunset eyes, personality changes — shunt malfunction can also cause lower extremity deterioration via brainstem compression.\n- **Syringomyelia**: Can coexist with or mimic tethered cord. Upper extremity weakness, scoliosis, pain, sensory changes.\n- **Chiari II malformation status**: Present in nearly all patients with myelomeningocele. Can become symptomatic with swallowing difficulty, stridor, apnea, upper extremity weakness.\n- **Bladder function baseline**: Establish the previous CIC schedule, volume per catheterization, continence status, UTI frequency. Urodynamics should be done regularly (baseline, then annual or with changes).\n- **Bowel program**: Constipation is common. Current bowel regimen (timed toileting, stimulant, Malone antegrade continence enema?).\n- **Orthopedic history**: Prior surgeries, current bracing, spinal deformity, hip stability (hip subluxation/dislocation common at L3-L4 level).\n- **Skin**: Insensate skin areas. Any pressure injuries from braces or wheelchair?\n- **Latex allergy**: All myelomeningocele patients should be treated as latex-sensitive (high prevalence due to repeated surgeries).\n- **Developmental and academic function**: Grade level, school performance, social participation.',
