      title: '+ Describe the workup for suspected tethered cord syndrome, including how to differentiate it from other causes of neurologic deterioration in myelomeningocele.',
      content: '**Primary Workup — MRI of the Spine**:\n- **MRI of the entire spine with and without contrast** is the study of choice.\n- Findings suggestive of tethered cord: low-lying conus medullaris (below L2 in children over 12 weeks), thickened filum terminale (>2 mm), dorsal displacement of the cord against the posterior thecal sac at the repair site, scar tissue tethering the cord to the dura.\n- **Important caveat**: In patients with repaired myelomeningocele, the MRI will ALWAYS show an anatomically low conus and tethered appearance. The diagnosis of **symptomatic** re-tethering is therefore **clinical** — based on progressive neurologic deterioration, NOT imaging alone.\n- MRI also evaluates for **syringomyelia** (fluid-filled cavity within the spinal cord), which can cause similar progressive deficits and may coexist with tethering.\n\n**Additional Studies**:\n1. **MRI Brain**: Evaluate VP shunt function (ventricle size compared to prior imaging), Chiari II malformation status. **Shunt malfunction can mimic tethered cord** because elevated intracranial pressure can cause brainstem compression affecting lower extremity function.\n2. **CT head (rapid shunt series)**: If acute shunt malfunction is suspected, CT is faster than MRI. Compare ventricle size to baseline.\n3. **Urodynamics**: Compare to baseline. Findings suggestive of neurologic deterioration include increasing detrusor pressures, new detrusor overactivity, decreased bladder compliance, increasing post-void residuals.\n4. **Spine X-rays**: Evaluate scoliosis progression (Cobb angle measurement), hip stability, progressive foot deformity.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis of Neurologic Deterioration**:\n1. **Tethered cord** (most likely) — progressive, subacute onset, pain + motor + bladder changes\n2. **VP shunt malfunction** — can be insidious; assess for headaches, papilledema, compare ventricle size\n3. **Symptomatic syringomyelia** — can cause upper extremity weakness, scoliosis, dissociated sensory loss; seen on MRI\n4. **Symptomatic Chiari II** — brainstem signs (swallowing, stridor, apnea), upper extremity findings\n5. **Orthopedic causes** — hip subluxation, progressive scoliosis causing pain and functional decline (but would not explain bladder changes or ascending sensory level)\n6. **Normal growth-related stretching** — growth spurts can unmask subclinical tethering\n\n**The ascending sensory level and new bladder incontinence in this patient make tethered cord the leading diagnosis and require urgent neurosurgical evaluation.**',
