      content: '**Pre-Operative Preparation**:\n1. **Latex-free protocol**: Mandatory for all surgical procedures in myelomeningocele patients. Alert anesthesia, OR staff, and all providers. Use non-latex gloves, tubing, and equipment throughout.\n2. **Baseline documentation**: Record detailed motor exam, sensory levels, urodynamic findings, and functional status (ambulation distance, FIM/WeeFIM scores) for post-operative comparison.\n3. **Neurosurgical planning**: Intraoperative neurophysiologic monitoring (SSEP, MEP, EMG) is recommended during untethering to minimize risk of iatrogenic nerve injury.\n4. **Anesthesia considerations**: Evaluate for difficult airway (Chiari malformation), VP shunt positioning, cardiac evaluation if needed.\n5. **Child Life preparation**: Age-appropriate surgical preparation — use dolls, picture books, and tours of the surgical area to reduce anxiety. This 8-year-old is old enough to understand simple explanations.\n\n**Post-Operative Rehabilitation**:\n1. **Positioning**: Flat bed rest for 48-72 hours post-operatively to allow dural healing and reduce CSF leak risk. Log-roll precautions.\n2. **Pain management**: Multimodal approach (acetaminophen, ketorolac, low-dose opioid PCA or nurse-controlled analgesia). Avoid constipation aggressively (docusate, senna) as straining increases intrathecal pressure.\n3. **Wound monitoring**: Daily assessment for CSF leak (clear drainage on dressing), wound dehiscence, and infection.\n4. **Mobilization**: Gradual progression from supine to seated (day 3-5), then to upright and ambulation per neurosurgical clearance. Orthotics refitted as needed.\n5. **Motor monitoring**: Serial lower extremity exams compared to pre-operative baseline. Some patients experience transient worsening before improvement.\n6. **Outpatient therapy**: Intensive PT and OT for 8-12 weeks post-operatively to regain functional mobility. Goal: return to pre-decline ambulatory status (community ambulation with AFOs and crutches).\n\n**Bladder Management**:\n1. **Continue CIC program**: Typically Q4-6 hour schedule. Adjust catheterization frequency based on post-operative urodynamics.\n2. **Post-operative urodynamics**: Repeat at 3-6 months after surgery to assess for improvement in bladder compliance and capacity.\n3. **Anticholinergic medication**: If detrusor overactivity persists, continue or initiate **oxybutynin** (0.2 mg/kg/dose BID-TID) or **solifenacin** (dose adjusted for weight).\n4. **Renal protection**: Maintain detrusor leak point pressure below 40 cmH2O to protect the upper urinary tracts. Annual renal ultrasound.\n5. **UTI prevention**: Adequate fluid intake, complete bladder emptying with CIC, cranberry prophylaxis (limited evidence). Antibiotic prophylaxis only if recurrent febrile UTIs.\n6. **Bowel program**: Continue timed toileting or bowel program. Consider antegrade continence enema (Malone/MACE procedure) if bowel incontinence is significantly impacting quality of life.',
